---
// Traduction anglaise de /management-de-transition/ le lien "Notre approche
// management de transition" de la home EN pointe ici.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/legacy-article.css";

const title = "Interim Management: Operational Leadership";
const description =
  "Interim management for mid-market companies and scale-ups: an operational executive within weeks, measurable results from day 30, guaranteed independence. Get in touch.";

const serviceSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  serviceType: "Interim Management",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: "https://izybiz.fr/en/interim-management/",
  areaServed: "France",
  provider: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Izybiz",
    url: "https://izybiz.fr",
  },
};
---

<Layout lang="en" title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/en/interim-management/">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <main class="legacy-article">
    <article class="legacy-article__article">
      <a class="legacy-article__back" href="/en/">Home</a>
      <h1 class="legacy-article__title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="legacy-article__lede">{description}</p>

      <div class="legacy-article__body">
        <p>
          Not a consultant who comes back at month-end with a deck. An executive
          who takes charge from the first week, with your teams, on your actual
          challenges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Interim management is the answer when you need a level of seniority you
          don't have in-house and can't afford to wait 6 months to recruit it.
        </p>
        <p class="legacy-article__cta-line">
          <a class="legacy-article__cta" href="/en/contact/" data-cta="mid-page">Discuss your situation</a>
        </p>


        <h2>When interim management is the right answer</h2>
        <p>
          Some situations can't be handled with internal resources or a consulting
          firm looking on from above. They require a senior executive who commits
          to execution.
        </p>
        <p>You need an interim manager if:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A leadership role is vacant</strong> and operational continuity is at stake every week without a pilot costs you.</li>
          <li><strong>A transformation is derailing</strong> budget, timeline, results and the teams need a firm hand.</li>
          <li><strong>You're preparing a Series B</strong> and your processes won't hold the scale-up without being restructured first.</li>
          <li><strong>A business unit is struggling</strong> accelerating churn, collapsing margins, tightening cash.</li>
          <li><strong>You're integrating AI into your operations</strong> and projects keep ending as POCs with no follow-through, for lack of governance.</li>
          <li><strong>A strategic project is starting</strong> market launch, M&A, delivery overhaul and you need an experienced conductor.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In all these cases, interim management gives you access to an executive
          available immediately, with directly applicable experience, for a
          bounded duration.
        </p>


        <h2>What interim management is not</h2>
        <p>Interim management is not consulting. It's not coaching or mentoring either.</p>
        <p>
          It's taking on an operational role. The interim manager steps into a
          role CEO, deputy CEO, CFO, CDO, COO, strategic project lead and
          actually performs it, with your teams, day to day.
        </p>
        <p>They don't produce a report. They lead.</p>


        <h2>What Izybiz brings to an interim management mission</h2>

        <h3>Directly applicable seniority</h3>
        <p>
          30 years of leadership in tech technical manager first, then business
          unit director, then CEO. Software, Retail, E-commerce, Last-mile,
          Telecommunications. Commercial development, team mergers,
          transformation projects delivered in-house or as an external partner:
          Crédit Agricole, Bouygues Telecom, SFR, Canal+, SNCF, PSA, Carrefour,
          OVH, Engie, and startups.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you bring in Izybiz, you're working with someone who has already
          managed what you're going through not someone learning at your
          expense.
        </p>

        <h3>Operational from week one</h3>
        <p>
          Interim management only has value if it produces measurable results
          quickly. The method: scoping in Day 0-15, first levers actioned by Day
          30, formal check-in with the board by Day 60.
        </p>
        <p>The mission's KPIs are defined during initial scoping. No ambiguity about what's expected.</p>

        <h3>Independence on exit</h3>
        <p>
          At the end of the mission, your organisation is autonomous. No
          dependency created, no continuation of services sold on the way out.
          The interim manager documents, trains, structures and leaves.
        </p>


        <h2>Interim management vs. the alternatives</h2>

        <h3>vs. Consulting firm</h3>
        <table class="legacy-article__table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Traditional consulting</th><th>Izybiz interim management</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Deliverable</th><td>Recommendations, slides</td><td>Operational execution</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Presence</th><td>Occasional meetings</td><td>In the teams, day to day</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cost</th><td>Very high (junior team + overhead)</td><td>Significantly lower</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Skin in the game</th><td>No</td><td>Yes the manager owns the results</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Dependency created</th><td>Often</td><td>No exits fully independent</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>vs. Permanent hire</h3>
        <table class="legacy-article__table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Permanent hire</th><th>Izybiz interim management</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Timeline</th><td>6 to 12 months</td><td>A few weeks</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Risk</th><td>High (a bad hire = 18 months lost)</td><td>Low bounded mission, clear objectives</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Total cost</th><td>Very high (recruitment + salary + charges)</td><td>Predictable, per mission</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Results</th><td>Uncertain, long learning curve</td><td>Measurable from Day 30</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Fit</th><td>Uncertain before 6 months</td><td>Validated at scoping</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>vs. Other interim managers</h3>
        <p>The Izybiz difference comes down to three things:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Dual expertise</strong>: classic operational transformation + AI integration in business. Few interim managers can operate both.</li>
          <li><strong>Verifiable references</strong> across several tech sectors not generic references.</li>
          <li><strong>A structured, documented approach</strong> not artisanal. The mission leaves a trail your teams can pick up.</li>
        </ol>


        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <h3>What's the minimum duration of an interim management mission?</h3>
        <p>
          Missions generally start at 3 months. Below that, it's difficult to
          scope, execute and leave behind a stable organisation. Standard
          duration is 6 to 12 months, with a possible extension if the stakes
          require it.
        </p>

        <h3>How does a mission start?</h3>
        <p>
          The first week is dedicated to diagnosis and scoping: understanding the
          stakes, auditing teams and processes, defining mission KPIs. First
          levers are actioned by Day 15-30. A formal check-in with the board or
          shareholders is organised by Day 60.
        </p>

        <h3>What does an interim management mission cost?</h3>
        <p>
          Missions are billed daily or as a fixed fee depending on the nature of
          the engagement. There's no public pricing each mission is scoped
          individually based on the stakes, duration and scope.
        </p>

        <h3>Interim manager or permanent hire: which to choose?</h3>
        <p>
          If you need to close a gap quickly (an ongoing transformation, a vacant
          leadership role, a critical project) and the need is time-bound,
          interim management is the answer. If you have 6 months ahead of you, a
          stable situation and a permanent need, a permanent hire is more
          suitable. In crisis or transformation situations, the two timelines
          aren't comparable.
        </p>

        <h3>Can you work outside France?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. Missions have included deployments in West Africa, Europe and the
          Middle East. The working language is adapted to context.
        </p>

        <h3>How is a mission's ROI measured?</h3>
        <p>
          KPIs are defined during initial scoping. They're tracked monthly. At
          the end of the mission, every result is documented and traceable. ROI
          is measurable that's a requirement, not an option.
        </p>


        <h2>Ready to talk?</h2>
        <p>
          A situation can't be summed up in a few lines. Book a 30-minute call
          no sales pitch, no commitment.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 30 minutes, we clarify whether your situation calls for interim
          management and whether Izybiz is the right fit to deliver it.
        </p>
        <p class="legacy-article__cta-line">
          <a class="legacy-article__cta" href="/en/contact/" data-cta="bottom-cta">Book a call</a>
        </p>
        <p><em>Available within weeks. Measurable results from Day 30.</em></p>


        <p class="legacy-article__footnote">
          <em>See also: <a href="/en/ai-integration/">AI integration in business</a>
          for situations where operational transformation comes with an AI
          challenge.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
